From: Reindert Pelsma <reindert.pelsma@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 17:10:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] ui/gtk: make the pointer grab switch the guest's
 pointing device

*** THIS PATCH IS NOT KNOWN TO WORK.  The mechanism it automates was
*** tried by hand on real hardware -- `mouse_set <relative index>` from
*** the monitor, then take the grab -- and mouse look in the guest still
*** did not move.  Read "What the hardware said" below before merging it
*** anywhere that matters.  It is on gpu-untested for that reason.

Ctrl+Alt+G confines the host cursor and changes nothing else, so the
guest keeps receiving whatever kind of coordinates it was receiving
before.  With a virtio-tablet current that is ABSOLUTE coordinates, and
a tablet says "the pointer is at (x, y)", never "it moved by (dx, dy)",
so a guest reading deltas for pointer lock gets nothing.

gd_grab_pointer() takes the GDK seat grab, records the pointer position,
sets ptr_owner and updates the caption.  It never touches which input
handler is current.  QEMU keeps exactly one current mouse handler and it
is whichever was activated last (qemu_input_handler_activate() does
QTAILQ_INSERT_HEAD, qemu_input_find_handler() returns the first match),
and the GUEST picks that by enabling a device
(virtio_input_hid_change_active() activates on DRIVER_OK), so nothing on
the command line decides it either.

So: on grab, activate a relative device; on ungrab, put the absolute one
back.  Both directions go through the public interface --
qmp_query_mice() to read the list, qemu_mouse_set() to activate -- which
is exactly what the monitor's `info mice` and `mouse_set` do, so nothing
new is exported from ui/input.c and the monitor sees the same state.

WHAT THE HARDWARE SAID.  Before the test box was returned, the switch
was performed by hand: `mouse_set` onto the relative virtio-mouse, then
grab.  Mouse look still did not work.  That is a negative result about
the whole approach, not about this code's details, and it is why the
patch header opens the way it does.  What it does NOT tell us is where
the chain breaks, because the manual test exercised the same host path
this patch automates and stopped at the same place.

Reading the path afterwards, the host side looks correct and the
remaining suspects are guest-side.  All of the following is READING, not
measurement, and is written down so the next person starts further along
rather than because it is established:

  * qemu_input_is_absolute() finds the head-most REL|ABS handler and
    tests its ABS bit, so after the switch it is correctly false;
  * gd_motion_event() then takes its relative branch and queues
    qemu_input_queue_rel() deltas -- but only while `s->last_set &&
    s->ptr_owner == vc`, i.e. only while grabbed, which was the case;
  * qemu_input_find_handler() prefers handlers bound to the event's
    console and falls back to unbound ones.  virtio-input devices are
    unbound unless `display=` is set, so the fallback loop applies and
    returns the head-most REL handler, which is the one just activated;
  * virtio_input_send() DROPS every event when !vinput->active, and
    active tracks VIRTIO_CONFIG_S_DRIVER_OK.  A device the guest has not
    driven swallows input silently.  In the guest under test both event
    nodes existed (pci-0000:00:09.0-event-mouse relative,
    pci-0000:00:0a.0-event-mouse tablet), which says the driver probed
    both, so this is probably not it -- but it is the one place in the
    host chain that can fail without a symptom;
  * that leaves the guest's own stack.  It runs weston with Xwayland as
    a rootful client.  Two things there are worth suspecting before
    anything else: whether libinput/weston is driving the seat pointer
    from the tablet alone and treating the relative node as a device it
    has bound but is not using, and whether the application's pointer
    lock resolves at all under rootful Xwayland, which needs
    zwp_pointer_constraints_v1 / relative_pointer_v1 from the
    compositor.  If the second is the problem then no host-side change
    can fix it, and the delta source was never the question.

THE ONE TEST THAT SPLITS THIS, and it needs no game: in the guest, while
grabbed, run
    sudo evtest /dev/input/by-path/pci-0000:00:09.0-event-mouse
and move the mouse.  REL_X/REL_Y events mean the entire QEMU-side chain
including this patch works and the bug is above evdev, in
libinput/weston/Xwayland.  Silence means the break is at or below
virtio-input and this patch is looking in the right place but is not
enough.  Everything else should wait on that answer.

Edge cases handled, none of them measured either:

  * NO RELATIVE DEVICE, which is the default tablet-only config: the
    lookup returns 0 and grab never calls qemu_mouse_set().  Not an
    error path -- byte for byte the old behaviour, in both directions;
  * ALREADY RELATIVE when the grab is taken: nothing to switch, and
    nothing saved to restore;
  * NO ABSOLUTE DEVICE: ungrab finds no candidate and leaves the guest
    alone, which is right for a relative-only guest;
  * RESTORE WHAT WAS CURRENT, not merely "an absolute device".  Grab
    saves the current index and ungrab re-activates that exact one.  It
    is saved only when it was absolute, because restoring a relative
    device on ungrab would defeat the point.  With nothing saved, or
    with the saved device unplugged since (qemu_mouse_set() then fails),
    ungrab falls back to any absolute device;
  * TWO DEVICES OF A KIND: take the one closest to the head of the
    handler list, i.e. the most recently activated.  qmp_query_mice()
    prepends, so that is the LAST match in its list.

Interaction with the existing auto-ungrab in gd_mouse_mode_change()
("release the grab at switching to absolute mode"), which both
directions fire re-entrantly from inside qemu_input_handler_activate():

  * on grab we activate a RELATIVE device, so qemu_input_is_absolute()
    is false when the notifier runs and its body is skipped.  The switch
    happens after ptr_owner is set, so the notifier sees a consistent
    owner;
  * on ungrab we activate an ABSOLUTE device, which is what that
    notifier reacts to -- but ptr_owner has already been cleared, so its
    `if (s->ptr_owner && ...)` is false.  No second ungrab, no loop;
  * the notifier's own trigger still works.  When the GUEST goes
    absolute mid-grab it ungrabs as before, and the restore then finds
    an already-absolute device and returns without calling
    qemu_mouse_set().  That guard is what stops a re-entrant activate,
    and it also stops a stale saved index from overriding the guest.

One incidental change on the grab path: s->last_set is cleared.
gd_motion_event() derives deltas from s->last_x/last_y, which until the
grab were written by absolute motion events -- and no motion event
arrives while the pointer is outside the window, so they can be
arbitrarily stale.  Without this the first motion after a grab emits
that staleness as one large delta.  The edge-warp path in the same
function clears last_set for the same reason.

Known left open: if the window manager breaks the GDK grab, ui/gtk.c
already leaves ptr_owner stale, and now the guest is left on the
relative device too until the user toggles the grab twice.
gd_grab_broken_event() is Windows-only and does not ungrab even there,
so there is no hook to extend; that is its own change.

NOT VERIFIED, restated because it is the point: no grab was taken on
this machine, no guest saw a delta, `info mice` was never run against a
QEMU carrying this patch.  It has a clean --enable-gtk build
(NVKVM_QEMU_UI=1, the only configuration that compiles a line of this
file) with no new warnings, and that is all.

NOT UPSTREAMABLE AS WRITTEN.  The gap is general -- upstream's grab is a
host-side window concept with no opinion about which guest device should
be current, and every front end that grabs has the same problem; ui/sdl2.c
has its own grab and is untouched here, so the two backends now differ.
The upstreamable version is a shared helper in ui/input.c that front ends
call, with the mode choice expressed once instead of per backend.
---
 ui/gtk.c | 135 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 135 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ui/gtk.c b/ui/gtk.c
index 0300dc5c9f..60808d9eb0 100644
--- a/ui/gtk.c
+++ b/ui/gtk.c
@@ -35,6 +35,7 @@
 #include "qapi/qapi-commands-control.h"
 #include "qapi/qapi-commands-machine.h"
 #include "qapi/qapi-commands-misc.h"
+#include "qapi/qapi-commands-ui.h"
 #include "qemu/cutils.h"
 #include "qemu/error-report.h"
 #include "qemu/main-loop.h"
@@ -1755,6 +1756,134 @@ static void gd_ungrab_keyboard(GtkDisplayState *s)
     trace_gd_ungrab(vc->label, "kbd");
 }
 
+/*
+ * nvkvm: make the grab decide which pointing device the guest sees.
+ *
+ * gd_grab_pointer() confines the host cursor but never touches which mouse
+ * handler is current, so a grab taken while a tablet is current still delivers
+ * ABSOLUTE coordinates and a guest that takes a pointer lock reads no deltas.
+ * Grab makes the relative device current; ungrab puts back the absolute one.
+ *
+ * READ THE COMMIT MESSAGE BEFORE TRUSTING THIS.  Doing the same switch by hand
+ * from the monitor (`mouse_set <relative index>`, then grab) was tried on real
+ * hardware and did NOT produce motion in the guest.  This code is the necessary
+ * half of the fix, not the demonstrated whole of it.
+ */
+
+/* Index of the absolute device to restore on ungrab; 0 = nothing saved. */
+static int nvkvm_gd_saved_mouse;
+
+/*
+ * One walk over the mouse list.  Returns the index of the device to make
+ * current for `absolute`, or 0 when the guest has none; *cur / *cur_abs
+ * describe whichever device is current right now (*cur is 0 when the guest has
+ * no mouse at all).
+ *
+ * qmp_query_mice() marks the HEAD of QEMU's handler list current and builds its
+ * answer by PREPENDING, so the list it hands back runs tail-to-head: the match
+ * closest to the head -- the most recently activated one, i.e. the device the
+ * guest most recently chose to enable -- is the LAST match in the list, not the
+ * first.  That is why the loop below overwrites `found` instead of stopping.
+ */
+static int nvkvm_gd_pick_mouse(bool absolute, int *cur, bool *cur_abs)
+{
+    MouseInfoList *mice, *entry;
+    int found = 0;
+
+    *cur = 0;
+    *cur_abs = false;
+
+    mice = qmp_query_mice(NULL);
+    for (entry = mice; entry != NULL; entry = entry->next) {
+        MouseInfo *m = entry->value;
+
+        if (m->current) {
+            *cur = (int)m->index;
+            *cur_abs = m->absolute;
+        }
+        if (m->absolute == absolute) {
+            found = (int)m->index;
+        }
+    }
+    qapi_free_MouseInfoList(mice);
+
+    return found;
+}
+
+static void nvkvm_gd_grab_set_relative(GtkDisplayState *s)
+{
+    bool cur_abs = false;
+    int cur = 0;
+    int rel;
+
+    nvkvm_gd_saved_mouse = 0;
+
+    rel = nvkvm_gd_pick_mouse(false, &cur, &cur_abs);
+    if (rel == 0 || rel == cur) {
+        /*
+         * No relative device at all -- the default tablet-only config -- or the
+         * guest already has one current.  Change nothing, so the grab behaves
+         * exactly as it did before this patch.
+         */
+        return;
+    }
+
+    if (!qemu_mouse_set(rel, NULL)) {
+        /* Raced with an unplug.  Keep whatever is current; a confined cursor
+         * is still better than a half-applied switch. */
+        return;
+    }
+
+    /* Only an absolute device is worth restoring by index; see
+     * nvkvm_gd_ungrab_set_absolute(). */
+    nvkvm_gd_saved_mouse = cur_abs ? cur : 0;
+
+    /*
+     * gd_motion_event() derives relative deltas from s->last_x/last_y, which
+     * up to now were written by ABSOLUTE motion events -- and no motion event
+     * arrives at all while the pointer is outside the window, so they can be
+     * arbitrarily stale.  Clearing last_set makes the first event after the
+     * grab seed the position instead of emitting that staleness as one huge
+     * delta, which the guest would render as the view snapping round.  The
+     * edge-warp path in gd_motion_event() clears it for the same reason.
+     */
+    s->last_set = FALSE;
+}
+
+static void nvkvm_gd_ungrab_set_absolute(void)
+{
+    bool cur_abs = false;
+    int cur = 0;
+    int want = nvkvm_gd_saved_mouse;
+    int abs_idx;
+
+    nvkvm_gd_saved_mouse = 0;
+
+    abs_idx = nvkvm_gd_pick_mouse(true, &cur, &cur_abs);
+    if (cur_abs) {
+        /*
+         * Already absolute -- and this is the case that matters for the
+         * auto-ungrab in gd_mouse_mode_change().  That notifier fires from
+         * INSIDE qemu_input_handler_activate() when the guest makes an absolute
+         * device current while we hold the grab, and it reaches this function
+         * through gd_ungrab_pointer().  Calling qemu_mouse_set() here would
+         * re-enter the notifier and undo the guest's own choice, so don't.
+         */
+        return;
+    }
+    if (want != 0 && qemu_mouse_set(want, NULL)) {
+        return;
+    }
+    /*
+     * Nothing was saved (the grab found the guest already relative), or the
+     * saved device has been unplugged since.  Fall back to any absolute device;
+     * with none -- a relative-only guest -- leave the guest alone, as before.
+     */
+    if (abs_idx != 0 && abs_idx != want) {
+        qemu_mouse_set(abs_idx, NULL);
+    }
+}
+
 static void gd_grab_pointer(VirtualConsole *vc, const char *reason)
 {
     GdkDisplay *display = gtk_widget_get_display(vc->gfx.drawing_area);
@@ -1771,6 +1900,9 @@ static void gd_grab_pointer(VirtualConsole *vc, const char *reason)
     gdk_device_get_position(gd_get_pointer(display),
                             NULL, &vc->s->grab_x_root, &vc->s->grab_y_root);
     vc->s->ptr_owner = vc;
+    /* nvkvm: after ptr_owner is set, so the mouse-mode notifier this can fire
+     * sees an owner and a relative console, and therefore does not ungrab. */
+    nvkvm_gd_grab_set_relative(vc->s);
     gd_update_caption(vc->s);
     trace_gd_grab(vc->label, "ptr", reason);
 }
@@ -1790,6 +1922,9 @@ static void gd_ungrab_pointer(GtkDisplayState *s)
     gdk_device_warp(gd_get_pointer(display),
                     gtk_widget_get_screen(vc->gfx.drawing_area),
                     vc->s->grab_x_root, vc->s->grab_y_root);
+    /* nvkvm: after ptr_owner is cleared above, so the mouse-mode notifier this
+     * can fire finds no owner and cannot recurse back into an ungrab. */
+    nvkvm_gd_ungrab_set_absolute();
     gd_update_caption(s);
     trace_gd_ungrab(vc->label, "ptr");
 }
